campaign.

510
00:28:47,564 --> 00:28:52,284
If this number is high, the objection changes from "nobody will touch this"

511
00:28:52,384 --> 00:28:56,884
to "who moves first, and how long do they pretend to think about it?".

512
00:28:58,004 --> 00:29:00,144
Why Delay Becomes Irrational.

513
00:29:01,144 --> 00:29:04,284
Once you believe a coalition is likely to form anyway,

514
00:29:05,064 --> 00:29:08,904
waiting stops looking prudent and starts looking like the time you didn't

515
00:29:08,924 --> 00:29:12,654
buy Apple stock because you wanted to "see how it plays out.".

516
00:29:13,124 --> 00:29:14,184
Move early:

517
00:29:14,344 --> 00:29:18,244
You define the structure, recruit the coalition, capture the largest returns,

518
00:29:18,764 --> 00:29:22,484
and get the Wikipedia entry that says "one of the people who ended most

519
00:29:22,484 --> 00:29:23,124
disease."

520
00:29:23,724 --> 00:29:26,824
Your grandchildren reference it at dinner parties forever.

521
00:29:27,284 --> 00:29:27,784
Wait:

522
00:29:28,204 --> 00:29:32,964
Someone else writes the terms, gets the credit, and locks in first-mover

523
00:29:32,964 --> 00:29:33,464
influence.

524
00:29:34,184 --> 00:29:38,004
You explain to your grandchildren that you were "monitoring the situation,"

525
00:29:38,324 --> 00:29:40,584
and they nod politely while looking at their phones.

526
00:29:41,464 --> 00:29:44,504
The question is no longer "Will anyone fund this?"

527
00:29:44,644 --> 00:29:49,124
It is "Do I want to be one of the four who shaped the largest health intervention

528
00:29:49,124 --> 00:29:53,484
in history, or the person who arrived after the structure was set and asked

529
00:29:53,504 --> 00:29:54,264
if there were still tickets?".

530
00:29:56,124 --> 00:29:57,284
What Would Have To Be Wrong.

531
00:29:58,144 --> 00:30:02,944
If no serious actor moves within ten years, then at least one of the following

532
00:30:02,944 --> 00:30:03,444
is true:

533
00:30:04,284 --> 00:30:04,984
One.

534
00:30:04,984 --> 00:30:06,584
The expected-value math is wrong.

535
00:30:07,044 --> 00:30:10,364
The R.O.I., health gains, or funding leverage do not hold.

536
00:30:10,904 --> 00:30:12,044
Check the math in the appendix.

537
00:30:12,964 --> 00:30:13,464
Two.

538
00:30:14,004 --> 00:30:16,254
The idea never reached enough decision-makers.

539
00:30:16,844 --> 00:30:19,064
The diffusion model failed in the real world.

540
00:30:19,544 --> 00:30:20,484
Three.

541
00:30:20,884 --> 00:30:23,364
The private-incentive bridge is weaker than modeled.

542
00:30:24,024 --> 00:30:28,404
The surplus exists, but not enough of it is capturable by first movers.

543
00:30:28,864 --> 00:30:29,624
Four.

544
00:30:30,204 --> 00:30:32,724
Hidden costs dominate the visible upside.

545
00:30:33,304 --> 00:30:38,084
Reputational risk, retaliation, legal friction, or coordination costs are

546
00:30:38,084 --> 00:30:38,944
larger than assumed.

547
00:30:40,264 --> 00:30:40,864
Five.

548
00:30:40,924 --> 00:30:44,824
Enough elite actors prefer inertia to asymmetrical upside.

549
00:30:45,384 --> 00:30:45,884
Possible.

550
00:30:46,224 --> 00:30:50,844
But across a plausible aligned tail of thirty principals inside a broader

551
00:30:50,844 --> 00:30:55,284
universe of two thousand nine hundred and eighty implementers over ten years,

552
00:30:55,724 --> 00:30:59,144
it is not the first explanation a rational model should reach for.

553
00:31:00,264 --> 00:31:03,654
Your task, if you want to reject this, is to pick one.

554
00:31:04,104 --> 00:31:08,224
Not to gesture vaguely at "complexity," which is what humans say when they

555
00:31:08,224 --> 00:31:09,984
mean "I don't want to think about it."

556
00:31:10,584 --> 00:31:13,844
Pick the failure mode. Show your work.

557
00:31:13,844 --> 00:31:15,484
Your species demands this of schoolchildren.

558
00:31:16,344 --> 00:31:19,434
Surely you can manage it for a question about whether to cure diseases.

559
00:31:21,184 --> 00:31:23,184
Why This Still Feels Inevitable.

560
00:31:23,864 --> 00:31:25,604
This is still a probabilistic story.

561
00:31:26,484 --> 00:31:30,444
But once diffusion and incentives are combined, it stops looking like a

562
00:31:30,444 --> 00:31:34,464
fragile moonshot and starts looking like a machine that is hard to keep

563
00:31:34,464 --> 00:31:34,964
dormant.

564
00:31:35,484 --> 00:31:37,524
The chain reaction model handles reach.

565
00:31:38,124 --> 00:31:39,604
The incentive design handles motion.

566
00:31:40,984 --> 00:31:43,424
The question stops being "can this happen?"

567
00:31:43,624 --> 00:31:48,024
and starts being "how long can everyone with money, ambition, and pattern

568
00:31:48,024 --> 00:31:51,304
recognition keep pretending this is not in front of them?".

569
00:31:52,344 --> 00:31:57,854
Your species has a peculiar habit of treating path dependence as optional.

570
00:31:57,924 --> 00:31:58,424
It isn't.

571
00:31:58,784 --> 00:32:03,224
Once a mechanism pays people to expand it, the default trajectory changes.

572
00:32:03,784 --> 00:32:07,924
This is how you built railroads, credit markets, social networks, and defense

573
00:32:07,924 --> 00:32:08,684
industries.

574
00:32:09,164 --> 00:32:13,624
A system that pays people to fund health instead of weapons is not a proof.

575
00:32:14,204 --> 00:32:16,064
It is something more dangerous:

576
00:32:16,504 --> 00:32:20,084
a machine with incentives pointing in the right direction.

577
00:32:21,184 --> 00:32:25,584
Why The Treaty Won't Stop at one percent Most international commitments

578
00:32:25,584 --> 00:32:27,324
stagnate after signing.

579
00:32:27,984 --> 00:32:29,284
You saw the depressing table.

580
00:32:29,844 --> 00:32:31,644
zero point seven percent O.D.A.

581
00:32:31,904 --> 00:32:33,864
targets unmet for fifty-plus years.

582
00:32:34,624 --> 00:32:35,464
Kyoto abandoned.

583
00:32:35,884 --> 00:32:36,464
Paris missed.

584
00:32:37,144 --> 00:32:41,324
Your track record with follow-through makes goldfish look disciplined.

585
00:32:41,324 --> 00:32:44,224
So why would a one percent health treaty be any different?

586
00:32:45,004 --> 00:32:47,934
Because this time, people get paid.

587
00:32:48,204 --> 00:32:53,244
The eighty-ten-ten funding structure allocates ten percent of treaty funding

588
00:32:53,244 --> 00:32:57,884
(two point seven two billion dollars per year at one percent) to Incentive

589
00:32:57,884 --> 00:32:58,924
Alignment Bonds.

590
00:32:59,504 --> 00:33:03,964
These are securities that pay out when treaties *expand*, not just maintain.

591
00:33:04,624 --> 00:33:07,524
People get rich when more diseases get cured.

592
00:33:08,004 --> 00:33:12,044
It only took your species four hundred years of financial instruments to

593
00:33:12,104 --> 00:33:15,284
produce one that doesn't require someone to suffer (progress).

594
00:33:16,384 --> 00:33:18,224
The Expansion Mechanism.

595
00:33:18,284 --> 00:33:21,584
At a one percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B.

596
00:33:21,764 --> 00:33:26,484
pool is two point seven two billion dollars per year, creating strong political

597
00:33:26,484 --> 00:33:29,304
pressure, ten times the largest N.G.O.

598
00:33:29,404 --> 00:33:29,904
budgets.

599
00:33:30,224 --> 00:33:32,984
At a two percent level, the annual I.A.B.

600
00:33:33,264 --> 00:33:38,124
pool is five point four four billion dollars per year, creating very strong

601
00:33:38,124 --> 00:33:40,624
political pressure at a nation-state level.

602
00:33:41,144 --> 00:33:43,904
At a five percent level, the annual I.A.B.

603
00:33:44,184 --> 00:33:48,824
pool is thirteen point six billion dollars per year, creating overwhelming

604
00:33:48,824 --> 00:33:52,784
political pressure equivalent to a major industry lobby.

605
00:33:53,344 --> 00:33:56,004
At a ten percent level, the annual I.A.B.

606
00:33:56,284 --> 00:34:00,564
pool is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year, which is probably

607
00:34:00,564 --> 00:34:02,894
irresistible and has no precedent.

608
00:34:03,664 --> 00:34:06,124
Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty.

609
00:34:07,284 --> 00:34:09,624
zero point seven percent O.D.A.

610
00:34:09,624 --> 00:34:12,864
failed because nobody made money when countries complied.

611
00:34:13,304 --> 00:34:16,244
The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance run on guilt.

612
00:34:16,564 --> 00:34:18,564
Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency.

613
00:34:19,144 --> 00:34:20,934
Guilt gets you to the gym in January.

614
00:34:21,664 --> 00:34:23,104
Greed gets you there in July.

615
00:34:23,864 --> 00:34:26,314
Guilt volunteers at the soup kitchen once.

616
00:34:27,004 --> 00:34:29,984
Greed builds the soup kitchen and names it after itself.

617
00:34:31,044 --> 00:34:32,384
This treaty runs on greed.

618
00:34:33,624 --> 00:34:36,904
The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

619
00:34:37,524 --> 00:34:42,164
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent,

620
00:34:42,464 --> 00:34:43,064
then ten percent.

621
00:34:43,684 --> 00:34:46,754
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party.

622
00:34:46,784 --> 00:34:50,984
They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

623
00:34:51,424 --> 00:34:52,964
They have Bloomberg terminals.

624
00:34:53,144 --> 00:34:53,704
They have lobbyists.

625
00:34:54,304 --> 00:34:58,324
They have the same tools that weapons manufacturers currently use to expand

626
00:34:58,324 --> 00:35:02,904
military budgets, except pointed in the opposite direction.

627
00:35:03,284 --> 00:35:03,824
The Ratchet Effect.

628
00:35:05,124 --> 00:35:09,084
Once one percent passes, two point seven two billion dollars per year flows

629
00:35:09,084 --> 00:35:10,084
to I.A.B.

630
00:35:10,104 --> 00:35:11,464
holders pushing for two percent.

631
00:35:12,024 --> 00:35:16,264
When two percent passes, five point four four billion dollars per year pushes

632
00:35:16,264 --> 00:35:17,224
for five percent.

633
00:35:17,824 --> 00:35:22,284
The engine runs on compound greed, which is like compound interest but for

634
00:35:22,284 --> 00:35:23,024
not dying.

635
00:35:23,804 --> 00:35:25,144
The politicians feel it too.

636
00:35:25,604 --> 00:35:29,484
The treaty's ten percent political incentive allocation scales identically:

637
00:35:30,044 --> 00:35:33,784
at one percent, it's two point seven two billion dollars per year in campaign

638
00:35:33,784 --> 00:35:36,304
support for legislators who voted yes.

639
00:35:36,744 --> 00:35:37,524
At five percent,

640
00:35:38,144 --> 00:35:38,824
five times that.

641
00:35:39,324 --> 00:35:43,664
A politician who votes to expand the treaty is voting to expand their own

642
00:35:43,664 --> 00:35:44,744
reelection budget.

643
00:35:45,324 --> 00:35:47,474
You didn't change what politicians are.

644
00:35:47,824 --> 00:35:49,784
You changed what they're rewarded for.

645
00:35:50,604 --> 00:35:54,384
Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned by

646
00:35:54,384 --> 00:35:55,534
February.

647
00:35:55,683 --> 00:35:59,384
This one has a constituency that *grows* with each success.

648
00:36:00,024 --> 00:36:02,274
On Wishonia, we find this arrangement embarrassing.

649
00:36:03,024 --> 00:36:06,484
On Earth, it's the only arrangement that works.

650
00:36:06,984 --> 00:36:08,944
Long-Term Feasibility.

651
00:36:08,944 --> 00:36:15,044
With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political engine.

652
00:36:15,424 --> 00:36:18,183
A machine that runs on greed and outputs health.

653
00:36:19,473 --> 00:36:20,104
Embarrassing, but effective.

654
00:36:21,064 --> 00:36:25,744
The most effective things your species has ever built have all been embarrassing.

655
00:36:26,384 --> 00:36:30,163
The internet was built for sharing research papers and is used for arguing

656
00:36:30,163 --> 00:36:30,964
with strangers.

657
00:36:31,844 --> 00:36:36,384
Democracy was built for collective wisdom and is used for electing celebrities.

658
00:36:37,264 --> 00:36:41,704
This machine is built for curing disease and is powered by selfishness.

659
00:36:42,164 --> 00:36:43,204
The pattern holds.

660
00:36:43,744 --> 00:36:48,784
Without I.A.B.s, this joins Kyoto in the Museum of Things Humanity Promised

661
00:36:48,784 --> 00:36:49,284
to Do

662
00:36:49,824 --> 00:36:50,394
and Then Didn't.

663
00:36:51,104 --> 00:36:52,764
The museum is very large.

664
00:36:53,164 --> 00:36:53,884
It has a gift shop.

665
00:36:54,444 --> 00:36:56,064
The gift shop sells promises.

666
00:37:08,676 --> 00:37:14,216
Thank you so much for taking the time to end war and disease, but if at

667
00:37:14,216 --> 00:37:18,436
any point you decide you're too busy, please press the share button on your

668
00:37:18,436 --> 00:37:22,136
glowing rectangle in case one of your friends have a free afternoon to end

669
00:37:22,136 --> 00:37:23,176
war and disease instead.

670
00:37:23,796 --> 00:37:28,136
And if you're too busy to even do that, please leave a really, really, really

671
00:37:28,136 --> 00:37:32,296
good rating and review so we overcome the pro -war algorithmic suppression

672
00:37:32,296 --> 00:37:35,856
and find someone with more spare time for ending wars and diseases.

673
00:37:36,416 --> 00:37:38,616
The good review doesn't have to be true, they don't check.

674
00:37:39,156 --> 00:37:43,216
But if you're too busy to even do that, the absolute least you could do

675
00:37:43,216 --> 00:37:48,836
is take 15 seconds to go to WarOnDisease.org and make your wishocratic allocation,

676
00:37:49,256 --> 00:37:52,816
telling us what fraction of humanity's resources should go to explosions

677
00:37:52,816 --> 00:37:54,276
versus clinical trials.

678
00:37:54,556 --> 00:37:55,056
And vote!

679
00:37:55,476 --> 00:37:59,736
If the 1% treaty passes, your vote will be personally to blame for saving

680
00:37:59,736 --> 00:38:04,356
2.6 lives and preventing 468,000 hours of suffering.

681
00:38:04,356 --> 00:38:07,056
Go to WarOnDisease.org right now.

682
00:38:07,456 --> 00:38:08,016
Love you, bye!
